% ============================================================================
% plant-tropisms — the classic plant tropisms and the environmental stimulus
% each one grows toward (or away from), as the source names it
% (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A tropism is a directional growth movement of a plant in response to an
% environmental stimulus, and — as the source puts it — "Tropisms are usually
% named for the stimulus involved". So the name IS the answer key: a
% phototropism is a growth response to light, a gravitropism to gravity, a
% thigmotropism to touch, a hydrotropism to water, a chemotropism to chemicals.
% This is one of the first big ideas of plant biology, and a rung a BOTANY or
% ECOLOGY agent reasons UP from: before you can explain WHY a shoot bends to a
% window or a root dives downward, you must first know WHICH stimulus that
% tropism tracks. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? tropism_stimulus(phototropism, $Stimulus)      % light
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `tropism_stimulus(tropism, stimulus)` carrying THAT ROW'S OWN
% citation (RS-5e — before #14986 it was the table's single envelope),
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% stimulus WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not one of these tropisms (it never invents a stimulus).
%
% Each classic plant tropism and the stimulus the source states it responds to,
% as a little truth table:
%
%     tropism          stimulus         a beginner's cue
%     --------------   --------------   -------------------------------------
%     phototropism     light            shoots bend toward light
%     gravitropism     gravity          roots grow down, shoots grow up
%     thigmotropism    touch            tendrils coil around what they touch
%     hydrotropism     water            roots curve toward moisture
%     chemotropism     chemicals        pollen tubes grow toward a chemical cue
%     aerotropism      wind             plants grow toward or away from wind
%     electrotropism   electric_field   growth responds to an electric field
%     heliotropism     sun_direction    plant parts track the Sun's direction
%     magnetotropism   magnetic_fields  growth responds to magnetic fields
%     selenotropism    moon_direction   plant parts track the Moon's direction
%     thermotropism    temperature      growth responds to temperature
%     traumatotropism  wounding         orientation shifts after a wounding
%
% EXTENDED from 5 to 12 rows: the SAME already-cited Wikipedia "Tropism"
% article's own "Types of tropism" list names seven MORE tropisms beyond the
% original five, each with its own single-fact definition line — zero new
% source page needed.
%
% That extension claimed to be "WebFetch-verified, including a targeted second
% pass re-fetching all seven new terms' raw definition text directly". #14986
% checked the spans against a raw extraction and found that pass had produced
% at least one quote the page does not contain: it rendered
% "Electrotropism/Galvanotropism:" where the article says "Electrotropism, or
% galvanotropism:". The boast is removed rather than restated; the spans are
% now per-row, taken from a raw extraction, and pinned individually.
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a stimulus and recall its tropism:
%
%     ? tropism_stimulus($Tropism, gravity)   % gravitropism
%
% A word that is NOT one of these twelve tropisms — `inotropism` (a real term
% on the SAME Wikipedia page, but naming a MUSCLE's contraction response to
% drugs, not a plant's growth response to an environmental stimulus — the
% wrong domain entirely) — is deliberately NOT a row, so a tropism recall
% ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a stimulus. (The page's lead paragraph
% also uses "anemotropism" as its own naming-convention EXAMPLE for a
% wind-response, a different name for the same wind-response the page's own
% "Types of tropism" list separately calls "Aerotropism" — this table uses
% the types-list's own canonical name, `aerotropism`, mirroring how it
% already uses `gravitropism` rather than the page's noted synonym
% `geotropism`.) That honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe
% to build a reasoner on.
%
% The `stimulus` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be the
% stimulus word the source EXPLICITLY names for that tropism — never one the
% source does not support. (Where the source's phrasing carries an "s" or a
% plural — "lights or colors of light", "chemicals" — the atom uses the source's
% own stimulus noun in its singular head form: `light`, `chemicals`, `touch`,
% `water`, `gravity` — so every value stays literally checkable against the
% quoted span.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance. EVERY ROW CARRIES ITS OWN DEFINITION LINE, below, in the row's
% `{ ... }` block (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e) — read those, not a restatement here. One
% page, one tier, so no row restates `locator` or `trust`. The source is a
% Wikipedia article, a teaching-quality encyclopedia summary rather than a
% primary reference, so `trust consensus`.
%
% WHAT #14986 CHANGED. The envelope was the PHOTOTROPISM line, so a recall of
% `traumatotropism` came back warranted by a sentence about light. The envelope
% now carries the page's DEFINITION of a tropism, which warrants no row.
%
% IS EACH LINE ONE ELEMENT, OR A WELD? Checked before anything was built on
% them, because "Term: definition" is the shape that usually means a `<dt>`
% and a `<dd>` joined by an extractor. Here it is not: each definition is ONE
% rendered line — a single block element with inline markup inside it — so
% quoting it quotes one element. (A raw-HTML grep for a phrase finds nothing,
% because the markup sits between the words. That zero is the instrument, not
% the page.)
%
% TWO VARIANT HEADS, kept as the page writes them:
%
%   gravitropism    "Gravitropism (sometimes referred to as geotropism): is
%                    movement or growth in response to gravity"
%   electrotropism  "Electrotropism, or galvanotropism: the movement or growth
%                    in response to an electric field"
%
%   A probe that required a bare "Gravitropism:" head reported both rows as
%   having no definition line at all. That was the probe's prefix test.
%
% REFERENCE MARKERS ARE TRIMMED. The rendered line for chemotropism ends
% "...in response to chemicals[8]"; the [8] is a footnote superscript. Each
% span stops at the last word before the first bracket, which leaves a verbatim
% PREFIX of the page's own line — nothing reworded, and what is dropped is a
% marker, never content. A mutant that restores the bracket reddens.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table tropism_stimulus {
    columns tropism, stimulus

    row (phototropism, light) {
        % This line was the table's whole envelope, so it warranted
        % the other eleven rows too.
        source "Phototropism: movement or growth in response to lights or colors of light"
    }
    row (gravitropism, gravity) {
        % The page's head here is "Gravitropism (sometimes referred
        % to as geotropism):", not a bare "Gravitropism:". A probe
        % that required the bare form reported this row as having no
        % definition line at all -- the probe, not the page.
        source "Gravitropism (sometimes referred to as geotropism): is movement or growth in response to gravity"
    }
    row (thigmotropism, touch) {
        source "Thigmotropism: movement or growth in response to touch or contact"
    }
    row (hydrotropism, water) {
        source "Hydrotropism: movement or growth in response to water; in plants, the root cap senses differences in water moisture in the soil, and signals cellular changes that cause the root to curve towards the area of higher moisture"
    }
    row (chemotropism, chemicals) {
        % Every span in this table stops before the page's reference
        % markers ([8] here). That leaves a verbatim PREFIX of the
        % page's own line: nothing reworded, and what is dropped is a
        % footnote marker, never content.
        source "Chemotropism: the movement or growth in response to chemicals"
    }
    row (aerotropism, wind) {
        source "Aerotropism: the growth of plants towards or away from a source of wind"
    }
    row (electrotropism, electric_field) {
        % Variant head again: "Electrotropism, or galvanotropism:".
        source "Electrotropism, or galvanotropism: the movement or growth in response to an electric field"
    }
    row (heliotropism, sun_direction) {
        source "Heliotropism: the diurnal motion or seasonal motion of plant parts in response to the direction of the Sun, (e.g. the sunflower)"
    }
    row (magnetotropism, magnetic_fields) {
        source "Magnetotropism: movement or growth in response to magnetic fields"
    }
    row (selenotropism, moon_direction) {
        source "Selenotropism: motion of plant parts in response to the direction of the Moon"
    }
    row (thermotropism, temperature) {
        source "Thermotropism: movement or growth in response to temperature"
    }
    row (traumatotropism, wounding) {
        source "Traumatotropism: orientation deviation after suffering a wounding"
    }

    % The envelope is the FRAMING span -- the page's definition of what a
    % tropism IS. It defends the TABLE and warrants no row. Until #14986
    % it was the PHOTOTROPISM row's own line.
    source "In biology, a tropism is a phenomenon indicating the growth or turning movement of an organism, usually a plant, in response to an environmental stimulus."
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropism"
    trust consensus
}
